Forkspace creates lightweight, copy-on-write workspaces using overlayfs.

Each workspace shares the original directory as a read-only base while capturing all changes in a separate layer. Workspaces are disposable and switch in milliseconds.

Usage: forkspace <COMMAND>

Commands:
  fork     Create a new workspace from a directory
  drop     Remove a workspace and unmount its overlay
  enter    Open a shell (or run a command) inside a workspace
  run      Start a process in a workspace (dev server, Electron, etc.)
  switch   Switch the active workspace (hot-swap via proxy)
  ls       List all active workspaces
  diff     Show what changed in a workspace
  compare  Compare parallel workspace attempts side by side
  merge    Apply workspace changes back to the base directory
  pull     Bring base changes into a workspace
  land     Preserve a workspace's Git branch in the base repository
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
